##
    One name, two members.

    This program does not compile. A name belongs to one member of a type: two fields cannot
    share one, a field and a function cannot, and neither can two functions taking the same
    types. Versions of one function, told apart by what they take, are the exception and the
    only one.

    What makes this worth refusing is that nothing goes wrong at the point of the mistake. Every
    use of the name finds the first member declared, so the second sits there looking like code
    and running never — and the reader who wrote it watches their program do what the other one
    does.
##

model Shelf
    # Two fields. Reading 'count' would find the first, whatever the second says.
    integer count = 0;
    string count = "none";

    # A field beside a function. Worse than unreachable: the name would mean the field when
    # read and the function when called, deciding by whether parentheses happen to follow.
    public delegate(integer) report = function(i)
        Console.WriteLine(i);
    end function;

    public function report(integer i)
        Console.WriteLine(i);
    end function

    # Two functions taking the same types. There is nothing to choose between them, which the
    # compiler used to say only where one was called — and only if one ever was.
    public integer function Height()
        yield 1;
    end function

    public integer function Height()
        yield 2;
    end function

    # Versions of one function, told apart by what they take. These are correct, and are here
    # to say so: a rule that refused them would refuse the standard library.
    public function Add(integer copies)
        Console.WriteLine(copies);
    end function

    public function Add(string title)
        Console.WriteLine(title);
    end function
end model

shared model Program
    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("this program does not compile");
    end function
end model
